==English==
{{was wotd|2024|October|30}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)ker-|id=cut}}
The {{glossary|adjective}} is derived from Late {{inh|en|enm|scurvi}}, {{m|enm|scurvy}},<ref name="Lexico">{{R:Lexico|pos=n.'' and ''adj}}</ref> variants of {{m|enm|scurfi|t=having scurf, scabby}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=
scurfī|pos=adj|id=MED39093}}</ref> from {{m|enm|scurf|t=skin disease causing scabs or scales; flakes of skin that fall off due to a skin disease, etc.}}<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=scurf|pos=n|id=MED39091}}</ref> + {{m|enm|-i|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming adjectives}}.<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=-ī̆|pos=suf|id=MED21577}}</ref> {{m|enm||Scurf}} is derived from {{inh|en|ang|scurf}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*skurf-|t=to gnaw}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*(s)ker-|t=to cut off, sever; to divide, separate}}. {{surf|en|scurf|t1=skin disease; flakes of skin that fall off due to a skin disease; crust-like formations on the skin|-y|id2=adjectival|pos2={{glossary|suffix}} meaning ‘having the quality of’ forming adjectives}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=adj|id=5789152509|date=June 2024}}</ref>

The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from the adjective. It was used to translate the similar-sounding {{cog|nl|scheurbuik}}, {{cog|fr|scorbut}}, {{cog|gml|schorbūk|t=scurvy (disease)}}, etc.<ref name="Lexico"/><ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=3628927706|date=June 2024}}</ref>

